      en: "The Robert Louis Stevenson Museum is situated in Villa Vailima, the former residence of the renowned Scottish author who spent the final four years of his life in Samoa. Completed in 1890, the house reflects Stevenson's affinity for Victorian style blended with tropical adaptations. The author of 'Treasure Island' was revered by the local population as 'Tusitala' (Teller of Tales), and he was deeply involved in advocating for Samoan rights against colonial interventions. The museum houses an extensive collection of personal artifacts, first editions of his works, and furniture that document his life in Polynesia. Following his death in 1894, he was buried atop Mount Vaea, which overlooks the estate. The building later served as the residence for the German Governor and the New Zealand Administrator before being inaugurated as a museum in 1994. The beautifully landscaped gardens and historic ambiance make it one of the nation's most visited cultural landmarks and a pilgrimage site for literary enthusiasts worldwide.",
